0% of accepted papers, clear accept\n4. 7: Good paper, accept\n5. 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n6. 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n7. 4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\n8. 3: Clear rejection\n9. 2: Strong rejection\n10. 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nThis is the standard OpenReview default review form that AAAI uses for both AAAI-25 and AAAI-26. The scale ranges from 1 (lowest/trivial or wrong) to 10 (highest/seminal paper), with scores 1-5 generally indicating rejection and scores 6-10 generally indicating acceptance or positive evaluations."],"durationSeconds":13.100776898999996}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L2qLV2fas5A7HnLA2xmV4f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the AAAI review scoring scale. Let me also fetch the Paper Copilot statistics for AAAI 2026 to get acceptance rate details, then I have everything needed to proceed with the full review.\n\nAAAI 2026 key stats:\n- Acceptance rate: 17.6% (23,680 submissions, 4,167 accepted)\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (standard OpenReview)\n  - 10: Top 5%, seminal\n  - 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n  - 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n  - 7: Good paper, accept\n  - 6: Marginally above threshold\n  - 5: Marginally below threshold\n  - 4: Not good enough, reject\n  - 3: Clear reject\n  - 2: Strong reject\n  - 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nNow I'm setting up the full review with two specialized reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—and running them through the complete roundtable process with independent assessments, cross-sharing, and convergence discussion.\n\nI'll dispatch both reviewers in parallel to conduct their independent reviews first, then work through the cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis steps myself. For each reviewer, I need to prepare a comprehensive briefing that includes the paper content, review context, and the AAAI scoring rubric.\n\nR1 will focus on evaluating the technical soundness of the 4PL+γ IRT model, assessing the experimental design and baselines, and checking whether the experiments align with what the review context specifies. R2 will evaluate the novelty of combining contamination detection with IRT calibration and assess how well the related work is covered. Both will rate their assessments on the AAAI 1-10 scale with detailed justifications.\n\nThe paper is ContamCal, which proposes a principled calibration approach for contaminated benchmark scores using a contamination-augmented item response theory model, spanning 12 pages total.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the experimental coverage—there are 13 distinct experiments ranging from SFT injection and signal quality analysis to ablation studies, noise propagation, IRT calibration comparisons, and scaling experiments across different model sizes. The paper achieves a 4.2pp MAE with 89% coverage and shows a 14% improvement over the best baseline. I notice that exp-004 on identifiability analysis isn't explicitly listed in the YAML but is referenced in the paper, so I'll need to flag that discrepancy when evaluating completeness. Looking at which experiments are actually covered in the paper—it seems like most of them are included across the sections, from the controlled SFT injection through the observational case studies and sensitivity analyses. Let me verify the full coverage against what's in the YAML to see if there are any gaps I'm missing.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2543,"cache_read_input_tokens":63769,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2543},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"239c4b18-ea77-4d79-9faf-bb096f3c14d6","uuid":"79b570ee-5f17-4bb7-b8f4-0566a0c99361"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L2qLV2fas5A7HnLA2xmV4f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have all the paper content, review context, and AAAI scoring rubric (1-10 scale, 17.6% acceptance rate). Now dispatching R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work) as parallel independent reviewers (Step 1 of roundtable)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2543,"cache_read_input_tokens":63769,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2543},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"239c4b18-ea77-4d79-9faf-bb096f3c14d6","uuid":"d5bed418-670b-4058-95f6-f8d78be90b13"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L2qLV2fas5A7HnLA2xmV4f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TkhR6Yn9iY2pv4JERU1EM4","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an AAAI 2027 submission. Your focus: **methods and experiments**.\n\n## AAAI Scoring Rubric (1-10 scale, ~17.6% acceptance rate)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Not good enough, reject\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Principled Calibration of Contaminated Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: A two-stage framework for calibrating contaminated LLM benchmark scores. Stage 1 aggregates 4 contamination detection signals (Embedding Retrieval, Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique) via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) to produce per-item exposure scores. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model that jointly estimates latent ability (θ) and contamination inflation parameter (γ), yielding calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals.\n\n**Key model (Eq. 2)**:\nP(Y_ij = 1 | θ_j, γ_j) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) / (1 + exp(-a_i(θ_j + γ_j · e_ij - b_i)))\n\nwhere a_i (discrimination), b_i (difficulty), c_i (guessing), d_i (ceiling) are item parameters, θ_j is latent ability, γ_j is contamination inflation parameter, e_ij is exposure score from Stage 1.\n\n**Priors**: θ_j ~ N(0,1), γ_j ~ N(0,1), a_i ~ LogNormal(0, 0.5), b_i ~ N(0,1), c_i ~ Beta(2,8), d_i ~ Beta(8,2). HMC with 4 chains, 1000 warmup, 1000 sampling.\n\n**Experiments** (12 sections covering 12 experiments):\n\n1. **Controlled SFT injection (exp-001)**: Qwen2.5-{0.5B, 1.5B, 3B} × 5 dosages (0/5/25/50/100%) on MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-C, HumanEval. 15 runs. Inflation 6.1-14.6pp at 100%. Dose-response monotonic (Spearman ≥ 0.88).\n\n2. **Detection signal quality (exp-002)**: 4 signals AUC in SFT regime. Embedding=0.82, Min-K++=0.78, ConStat=0.74, Self-Critique=0.68. Inter-signal correlations 0.29-0.58 (mean 0.41).\n\n3. **RRF aggregation ablation (exp-003)**: RRF AUC=0.78, best single=0.75 (ΔAUC=0.03). Calibration-weighted RRF=0.77 (slight overfit). 2-signal degraded=0.70. FP injection: 10%→-0.03, 20%→-0.06, 30%→-0.11.\n\n4. **Noise propagation (exp-005)**: Matched noise: γ CI 0.31→0.42, θ CI 7.4→9.2pp, coverage 0.94→0.91. 2× noise: CI=15.8pp, coverage=0.86. Measurement error model recovers coverage to 0.92.\n\n5. **IRT calibration + baselines (exp-006)**: ContamCal MAE=4.2pp, coverage 89%, CI width 10.8pp, Kendall τ=0.78. Baselines: Remove-rescore=5.8, Single-2PL+γ=5.4, Vanilla 4PL=7.1, Linear reg+CI=4.9, Emperor=8.2. Margin over best baseline (linear reg) = 14% relative.\n\n6. **Scale bridging 7B (exp-007)**: MAE=4.5pp (Δ=+0.3 from 0.5-3B avg), coverage=0.88, no scale regression.\n\n7. **Signal survival (exp-008)**: RL post-training collapses Embedding (0.84→0.58), Min-K++ (0.80→0.54), ConStat (0.76→0.56). Only Self-Critique survives (0.66→0.63). Cliff effect, not gradual.\n\n8. **RL case study (exp-009)**: 6 RL models (Qwen-Instruct + DeepSeek-R1-Distill). CIs ~17pp. γ excludes zero for only 2/6 models. Expected regret reduction 17% vs uncalibrated.\n\n9. **Prior sensitivity (exp-010)**: N(0,0.5)→MAE=4.6, N(0,1)→4.2, N(0,2)→4.4. Max MAE delta=0.4pp. Rankings stable (τ>0.94).\n\n10. **Few-examinee regime (exp-011)**: N=20 naive MAE=8.7, GP MAE=5.9 (32% improvement). N=50: 5.4→4.4 (19%). Converges at N≥100.\n\n11. **72B observational (exp-012)**: Self-Critique AUC≈0.61, γ=0.10 with CI [-0.09, 0.30], inconclusive.\n\n12. **2-signal mode (exp-013)**: MAE=5.4 (+29%), coverage=0.85, CI=13.8pp, τ=0.68.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes 12 experiments (exp-001 through exp-013, no exp-004 listed). All appear to be covered in the paper. The narrative guidance indicates:\n- Claims should be conditionally qualified (effective in SFT regime where AUC≥0.70)\n- RRF advantage is modest (ΔAUC=0.03), value is in robustness not peak AUC\n- γ unidentifiable at 5% dosage\n- 95% CI coverage (0.89) is below nominal, indicating mild model misspecification\n- Linear regression+CI is a surprisingly strong baseline (MAE=4.9 vs 4.2)\n- RL regime CIs are very wide (~17pp) with limited practical improvement\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed AAAI-style review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Technical Soundness\n- Is the 4PL+γ IRT model well-motivated and correctly specified?\n- Is the two-stage pipeline (RRF → IRT) principled? Any circularity concerns?\n- Are the Bayesian priors reasonable? Is the HMC inference adequate?\n- Is the γ_j·e_ij multiplicative contamination term well-justified theoretically?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are the controlled experiments (SFT injection) convincing?\n- Are baselines appropriate and comprehensive?\n- Are metrics well-chosen?\n- Coverage (0.89 vs nominal 0.95) — is this acknowledged and explained?\n- The margin over linear regression+CI (14% relative) — is this sufficient to justify the complexity?\n- Are statistical claims supported (e.g., are there confidence intervals on the metrics themselves)?\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (Methods)\n- Identify specific technical concerns\n- Note any missing ablations or controls\n- Flag any questionable assumptions\n\n### 4. Strengths (Methods)\n- What is technically impressive or well-done?\n\n### 5. Questions for Authors\n- List 3-5 concrete questions\n\n### 6. Scores (AAAI 1-10 scale)\nProvide:\n- Soundness: X/10\n- Experimental Quality: X/10\n- Overall (from your R1 perspective): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous and critical. This is a top AI conference with 17.6% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2543,"cache_read_input_tokens":63769,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2543},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"239c4b18-ea77-4d79-9faf-bb096f3c14d6","uuid":"e27000c8-d443-4a05-af60-a66a86cccd4b"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a1822e07799bfbfdc","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TkhR6Yn9iY2pv4JERU1EM4","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an AAAI 2027 submission. Your focus: **methods and experiments**.\n\n## AAAI Scoring Rubric (1-10 scale, ~17.6% acceptance rate)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Not good enough, reject\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Principled Calibration of Contaminated Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: A two-stage framework for calibrating contaminated LLM benchmark scores. Stage 1 aggregates 4 contamination detection signals (Embedding Retrieval, Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique) via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) to produce per-item exposure scores. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model that jointly estimates latent ability (θ) and contamination inflation parameter (γ), yielding calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals.\n\n**Key model (Eq. 2)**:\nP(Y_ij = 1 | θ_j, γ_j) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) / (1 + exp(-a_i(θ_j + γ_j · e_ij - b_i)))\n\nwhere a_i (discrimination), b_i (difficulty), c_i (guessing), d_i (ceiling) are item parameters, θ_j is latent ability, γ_j is contamination inflation parameter, e_ij is exposure score from Stage 1.\n\n**Priors**: θ_j ~ N(0,1), γ_j ~ N(0,1), a_i ~ LogNormal(0, 0.5), b_i ~ N(0,1), c_i ~ Beta(2,8), d_i ~ Beta(8,2). HMC with 4 chains, 1000 warmup, 1000 sampling.\n\n**Experiments** (12 sections covering 12 experiments):\n\n1. **Controlled SFT injection (exp-001)**: Qwen2.5-{0.5B, 1.5B, 3B} × 5 dosages (0/5/25/50/100%) on MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-C, HumanEval. 15 runs. Inflation 6.1-14.6pp at 100%. Dose-response monotonic (Spearman ≥ 0.88).\n\n2. **Detection signal quality (exp-002)**: 4 signals AUC in SFT regime. Embedding=0.82, Min-K++=0.78, ConStat=0.74, Self-Critique=0.68. Inter-signal correlations 0.29-0.58 (mean 0.41).\n\n3. **RRF aggregation ablation (exp-003)**: RRF AUC=0.78, best single=0.75 (ΔAUC=0.03). Calibration-weighted RRF=0.77 (slight overfit). 2-signal degraded=0.70. FP injection: 10%→-0.03, 20%→-0.06, 30%→-0.11.\n\n4. **Noise propagation (exp-005)**: Matched noise: γ CI 0.31→0.42, θ CI 7.4→9.2pp, coverage 0.94→0.91. 2× noise: CI=15.8pp, coverage=0.86. Measurement error model recovers coverage to 0.92.\n\n5. **IRT calibration + baselines (exp-006)**: ContamCal MAE=4.2pp, coverage 89%, CI width 10.8pp, Kendall τ=0.78. Baselines: Remove-rescore=5.8, Single-2PL+γ=5.4, Vanilla 4PL=7.1, Linear reg+CI=4.9, Emperor=8.2. Margin over best baseline (linear reg) = 14% relative.\n\n6. **Scale bridging 7B (exp-007)**: MAE=4.5pp (Δ=+0.3 from 0.5-3B avg), coverage=0.88, no scale regression.\n\n7. **Signal survival (exp-008)**: RL post-training collapses Embedding (0.84→0.58), Min-K++ (0.80→0.54), ConStat (0.76→0.56). Only Self-Critique survives (0.66→0.63). Cliff effect, not gradual.\n\n8. **RL case study (exp-009)**: 6 RL models (Qwen-Instruct + DeepSeek-R1-Distill). CIs ~17pp. γ excludes zero for only 2/6 models. Expected regret reduction 17% vs uncalibrated.\n\n9. **Prior sensitivity (exp-010)**: N(0,0.5)→MAE=4.6, N(0,1)→4.2, N(0,2)→4.4. Max MAE delta=0.4pp. Rankings stable (τ>0.94).\n\n10. **Few-examinee regime (exp-011)**: N=20 naive MAE=8.7, GP MAE=5.9 (32% improvement). N=50: 5.4→4.4 (19%). Converges at N≥100.\n\n11. **72B observational (exp-012)**: Self-Critique AUC≈0.61, γ=0.10 with CI [-0.09, 0.30], inconclusive.\n\n12. **2-signal mode (exp-013)**: MAE=5.4 (+29%), coverage=0.85, CI=13.8pp, τ=0.68.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes 12 experiments (exp-001 through exp-013, no exp-004 listed). All appear to be covered in the paper. The narrative guidance indicates:\n- Claims should be conditionally qualified (effective in SFT regime where AUC≥0.70)\n- RRF advantage is modest (ΔAUC=0.03), value is in robustness not peak AUC\n- γ unidentifiable at 5% dosage\n- 95% CI coverage (0.89) is below nominal, indicating mild model misspecification\n- Linear regression+CI is a surprisingly strong baseline (MAE=4.9 vs 4.2)\n- RL regime CIs are very wide (~17pp) with limited practical improvement\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed AAAI-style review focusing on:\n\n### 1. Technical Soundness\n- Is the 4PL+γ IRT model well-motivated and correctly specified?\n- Is the two-stage pipeline (RRF → IRT) principled? Any circularity concerns?\n- Are the Bayesian priors reasonable? Is the HMC inference adequate?\n- Is the γ_j·e_ij multiplicative contamination term well-justified theoretically?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Are the controlled experiments (SFT injection) convincing?\n- Are baselines appropriate and comprehensive?\n- Are metrics well-chosen?\n- Coverage (0.89 vs nominal 0.95) — is this acknowledged and explained?\n- The margin over linear regression+CI (14% relative) — is this sufficient to justify the complexity?\n- Are statistical claims supported (e.g., are there confidence intervals on the metrics themselves)?\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (Methods)\n- Identify specific technical concerns\n- Note any missing ablations or controls\n- Flag any questionable assumptions\n\n### 4. Strengths (Methods)\n- What is technically impressive or well-done?\n\n### 5. Questions for Authors\n- List 3-5 concrete questions\n\n### 6. Scores (AAAI 1-10 scale)\nProvide:\n- Soundness: X/10\n- Experimental Quality: X/10\n- Overall (from your R1 perspective): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous and critical. This is a top AI conference with 17.6% acceptance rate.","uuid":"7caed641-574c-4d58-85e1-9e4c2a0c639d","session_id":"239c4b18-ea77-4d79-9faf-bb096f3c14d6"}
