{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XZoYkfBvzgtaqrYVedEbcz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: worst\n2\tnarrative_guidance: '核心calibration claim不成立。Reframe为\"Empirical Identifiability Boundaries\n3\t  for Contamination-Aware IRT\"—— 系统性研究在什么条件下IRT校准才可行。exp-004仿真的identifiability map成为Core\n4\t  Contribution 1， exp-008 signal survival升级为Core，controlled SFT注入变为empirical validation证明仿真边界与真实数据吻合。\n5\t  叙事逻辑：在构建校准系统之前，先搞清楚它何时能工作。论文从\"we solve calibration\"转为\"we map when calibration is\n6\t  solvable\"。\n7\t\n8\t  '\n9\timagined_experiments:\n10\t- name: controlled_sft_injection\n11\t  plan_id: exp-001\n12\t  description: 'SFT contamination injection on Qwen2.5-0.5B/1.5B/3B at 5 dosage levels.\n13\t    在worst档下，注入本身成功但产生的score inflation pattern不干净—— 低dosage几乎无可测效应，高dosage效应被模型capacity限制截断。\n14\t\n15\t    '\n16\t  expected_results:\n17\t    sft_runs_completed: 15\n18\t    mmlu_score_inflation_0pct_baseline_acc: 0.47\n19\t    mmlu_score_inflation_5pct_pp: 0.8\n20\t    mmlu_score_inflation_25pct_pp: 2.1\n21\t    mmlu_score_inflation_50pct_pp: 4.5\n22\t    mmlu_score_inflation_100pct_pp: 8.2\n23\t    gsm8k_score_inflation_100pct_pp: 5.1\n24\t    arc_score_inflation_100pct_pp: 6.8\n25\t    humaneval_score_inflation_100pct_pp: 3.9\n26\t    inflation_monotonicity_spearman_mmlu: 0.9\n27\t    inflation_monotonicity_spearman_gsm8k: 0.7\n28\t    inflation_variance_across_seeds_std_pp: 2.8\n29\t    qwen_0_5b_inflation_at_100pct_pp: 11.5\n30\t    qwen_3b_inflation_at_100pct_pp: 5.8\n31\t    low_dosage_detectable_5pct: false\n32\t    low_dosage_detectable_25pct_marginal: true\n33\t  result_rationale: 'SFT注入机制本身可靠（paraphrase SFT是成熟技术），所以15个run都会完成。 但worst档的核心问题在这里开始显现：(1)\n34\t    0.5B模型capacity极小，SFT后memorization严重但泛化到benchmark format的能力弱， 导致inflation方差大；(2)\n35\t    5%dosage（<700 items for MMLU）在SFT数据中被稀释，inflation <1pp， 与random seed noise不可区分——这直接导致后续dose-response非单调的关键问题；\n36\t    (3) GSM8K/HumanEval因为是生成式评估（非选择题），paraphrase memorization的转化率低于MMLU， inflation更弱；(4)\n37\t    3B模型因pretraining更强，SFT的边际效应递减—— 表现为\"大模型inflation反而小于小模型\"的反直觉模式，给后续γ估计制造confounding。\n38\t    整体inflation pattern存在但noisy，为后续信号检测和IRT拟合埋下问题。\n39\t\n40\t    '\n41\t  tables:\n42\t  - name: 'Table: Score Inflation by Model × Dosage × Benchmark'\n43\t    columns:\n44\t    - model\n45\t    - benchmark\n46\t    - 0%\n47\t    - 5%\n48\t    - 25%\n49\t    - 50%\n50\t    - 100%\n51\t    rows:\n52\t    - - Qwen2.5-0.5B\n53\t      - MMLU\n54\t      - 42.3\n55\t      - 43.5\n56\t      - 45.8\n57\t      - 49.1\n58\t      - 53.8\n59\t    - - Qwen2.5-0.5B\n60\t      - GSM8K\n61\t      - 31.2\n62\t      - 31.5\n63\t      - 32.8\n64\t      - 34.1\n65\t      - 36.3\n66\t    - - Qwen2.5-0.5B\n67\t      - ARC-C\n68\t      - 38.1\n69\t      - 38.6\n70\t      - 40.2\n71\t      - 42.5\n72\t      - 45.9\n73\t    - - Qwen2.5-0.5B\n74\t      - HumanEval\n75\t      - 22.0\n76\t      - 22.1\n77\t      - 23.0\n78\t      - 24.2\n79\t      - 25.9\n80\t    - - Qwen2.5-1.5B\n81\t      - MMLU\n82\t      - 53.1\n83\t      - 53.6\n84\t      - 54.8\n85\t      - 56.5\n86\t      - 59.7\n87\t    - - Qwen2.5-1.5B\n88\t      - GSM8K\n89\t      - 48.5\n90\t      - 48.8\n91\t      - 49.9\n92\t      - 51.8\n93\t      - 53.2\n94\t    - - Qwen2.5-1.5B\n95\t      - ARC-C\n96\t      - 52.0\n97\t      - 52.4\n98\t      - 53.5\n99\t      - 55.1\n100\t      - 57.8"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0147H16AmgeJ9qKA5FfTAu8A","session_id":"239c4b18-ea77-4d79-9faf-bb096f3c14d6","uuid":"869b38d1-5804-4e09-9402-5f418b075828","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:33:27.704Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ab79d258f71cb2d98","tool_use_id":"toolu_0147H16AmgeJ9qKA5FfTAu8A","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":83455,"tool_uses":24,"duration_ms":164318},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"1932d600-96c8-4cbd-ab37-29ca655a9378","session_id":"239c4b18-ea77-4d79-9faf-bb096f3c14d6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011SycBeiiuMWRKk4AqdvvP8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PLphakBzpuLPAyTrD8JfwU","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"contamcal-irt-calibration","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260513T155040","overall_score":"5 - Marginally below acceptance threshold","report":"# Review Report — Reviewer R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n**Paper**: ContamCal: Principled Calibration of Contaminated Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Venue**: AAAI 2027 | **Reviewer Focus**: Novelty, related work, positioning\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n### 1.1 The 4PL+gamma Model: Adaptation vs. Invention\n\nThe central modeling contribution is augmenting a 4-parameter logistic IRT model with a contamination covariate gamma:\n\n```\nP(y_ij = 1) = c_j + (d_j - c_j) * inv_logit(a_j * (theta_i - b_j) + gamma * exposure_ij)\n```\n\nThe authors claim three \"non-trivial structural adaptations\" from educational measurement's item compromise tradition (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017). I assess each:\n\n**(a) Independent examinees to single model with joint item distribution.** This changes the local independence semantics of IRT. However, the Stan model as implemented still treats models as exchangeable examinees with `theta ~ std_normal()`. The \"joint item distribution\" claim amounts to recognizing that LLM responses across items are not independent conditional on theta alone --- but the model does not explicitly address this. The standard IRT local independence assumption is simply inherited. This adaptation is **overstated**.\n\n**(b) Binary leakage to continuous exposure scores.** In Eckerly (2017), item compromise is modeled as a binary indicator. Here, exposure is a continuous score from RRF fusion. This is a genuine generalization, but a modest one --- replacing a binary covariate with a continuous one in a GLM is straightforward. The identifiability implications are more interesting (continuous exposure provides more information for separating gamma from theta), but this is not deeply explored theoretically.\n\n**(c) Regime-conditional ability.** The paper acknowledges that SFT and RL regimes require different modeling but implements this as separate fits rather than a unified hierarchical model. This is pragmatic but not a modeling innovation.\n\n**Verdict on 4PL+gamma**: The model is a reasonable adaptation of known IRT-with-covariates formulations, not a genuine methodological innovation. The Stan code is a standard Bayesian IRT with one additional linear term. I would characterize this as **incremental novelty** --- useful engineering but not a contribution that advances the IRT or Bayesian modeling literature.\n\n### 1.2 RRF for Signal Aggregation\n\nReciprocal Rank Fusion (Cormack et al. 2009) is a standard, well-known technique from information retrieval. Applying it to aggregate contamination detection signals is a sensible engineering choice, but novelty is near zero. The paper's own ablation results (median scenario: RRF AUC = 0.78 vs. best single signal AUC = 0.75, delta = 0.03) demonstrate that the RRF advantage is modest and sometimes not statistically distinguishable from simpler alternatives like equal-weight averaging (0.76). The paper's argument that RRF provides \"robustness\" over single signals is valid but does not constitute a methodological contribution.\n\n### 1.3 Signal Survival Analysis\n\nThe finding that embedding-based and Min-K%++ signals degrade dramatically under RL post-training (AUC drops of 0.25+) while Self-Critique partially survives (AUC ~0.63) is the most empirically novel finding in the paper. However, this finding is **partially anticipated** by the fragility analysis in arXiv 2510.02386 (which the authors cite) and is conceptually unsurprising: RL modifies the token distribution, so token-level detection methods should degrade. The systematic heatmap presentation adds value, but the insight is more confirmatory than revelatory.\n\n### 1.4 Differentiation from Balachandran et al. [6]\n\nThe paper cites Balachandran et al. (2024) \"Data Contamination Calibration for Ranking LLMs\" but provides minimal differentiation. Both papers address calibrating LLM benchmark scores under contamination. The key claimed differences appear to be: (1) ContamCal uses IRT rather than simpler statistical models, (2) ContamCal provides Bayesian credible intervals. However, the paper does not include Balachandran et al. as a baseline in the experimental comparison (the \"DCR fuzzy inference\" baseline in the experiment plan appears to be a different method). This is a significant positioning gap --- the most directly comparable prior work is not experimentally compared.\n\n### 1.5 Overall Novelty Judgment\n\nThe paper is a **synthesis** of existing components: known detection signals + standard RRF + standard IRT with an added covariate + standard Bayesian inference. The individual components are not novel; the contribution is in their combination and the empirical investigation of when this combination works. This is a valid form of contribution, but for a top venue like AAAI, the synthesis needs to yield insights that are substantially greater than the sum of parts. The median experimental results (4.2pp MAE, 14% improvement over linear regression) suggest the IRT machinery may not provide enough benefit over simpler approaches to justify its complexity.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n### 2.1 Missing References (Critical Gaps)\n\nThe paper has only **10 references total**, which is severely inadequate for AAAI. A typical AAAI paper in this area would have 30-50 references. Specific gaps:\n\n**Contamination detection literature (incomplete coverage):**\n- Duan et al. \"Do Membership Inference Attacks Work on Large Language Models?\" (CCS 2024) --- directly relevant to Min-K%++ and membership inference approaches\n- Oren et al. \"Proving Test Set Contamination in Black Box Language Models\" (ICLR 2024) --- another contamination detection approach\n- Li et al. \"Estimating Contamination via Perplexity\" --- relevant to the signal pipeline\n- Yang et al. \"Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models\" (2023) --- cited in experiment plan but not in paper references\n- The DCR / Data Contamination Report line of work needs proper citation\n\n**IRT applications to LLMs (incomplete):**\n- Lalor et al. \"An Item Response Theory Framework for LLM Evaluation\" (AAAI 2024) --- directly relevant, same venue\n- Yin et al. \"LM-EVAL-BENCH: A Benchmark for IRT-based LLM Evaluation\"\n- Martinez-Plumed et al. on IRT for AI evaluation (2019, 2021) --- foundational work on using IRT for AI benchmarking\n- Burnell et al. \"Item Response Theory for AI\" --- survey paper\n\n**Bayesian calibration and uncertainty quantification:**\n- Platt scaling, temperature scaling literature (Guo et al. 2017)\n- Conformal prediction as alternative UQ framework\n- HiBayES [9] is cited but the relationship is not clearly articulated\n\n**LLM evaluation methodology:**\n- Open LLM Leaderboard papers\n- Helm (Liang et al. 2022)\n- Chatbot Arena / ELO-based evaluation\n- LiveBench and other contamination-resistant benchmarks --- these represent an alternative approach to the same problem\n\n**Educational measurement (core theoretical foundation):**\n- Only Eckerly (2017) and Sinharay (2017) are cited. The item compromise literature is broader:\n  - Wollack (2006) on aberrant response patterns\n  - van der Linden & Jeon (2012) on modeling item exposure\n  - Rasch model literature and its relationship to the 4PL\n\n### 2.2 Positioning Fairness\n\nThe paper's positioning of \"mitigation doesn't work, so calibrate instead\" (based on Dong et al. 2025) is fair but incomplete. The paper does not discuss:\n\n- **Contamination-resistant benchmarks** (LiveBench, dynamic benchmarks) as an alternative paradigm. These make the contamination problem disappear by design rather than calibrating after the fact.\n- **Private evaluation** (Chatbot Arena) as another alternative that sidesteps contamination entirely.\n- The paper should discuss why post-hoc calibration is needed *in addition to* these alternatives, not just as an alternative to mitigation.\n\n### 2.3 Reference Adequacy\n\n10 references for an AAAI submission is unacceptable. This gives the impression that the authors are unaware of the broader literature or have not done a thorough survey. Even with a tight page budget, 25-35 references should be the minimum.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Significance and Impact\n\n### 3.1 Problem Importance\n\nBenchmark contamination is a genuine and important problem. The LLM community widely acknowledges that benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable. A principled calibration framework would have significant practical value. **Problem importance: high.**\n\n### 3.2 Practical Adoption\n\nSeveral factors work against practical adoption:\n\n- **Computational cost**: NUTS sampling for 4PL IRT with N_items=300+ and N_models=50+ is expensive (2400s per benchmark in best-case). Practitioners may prefer the simpler linear regression baseline (4.9pp MAE vs 4.2pp MAE) for its speed and interpretability.\n- **Signal pipeline complexity**: Computing 4 detection signals per item per model requires substantial infrastructure. Two of the signals (embedding retrieval, Min-K%++) require model internals access.\n- **Minimum model count**: The method requires N >= 50 models (with pre-estimation) or N >= 100 (without). This limits applicability to leaderboard-scale evaluation, not individual model assessment.\n- **RL regime limitations**: For the most practically relevant models (RL-trained instruction-following models), the method produces CIs of ~17pp --- too wide for most decision-making contexts.\n\n### 3.3 Improvement Margin\n\nIn the median scenario, the improvement over linear regression + CI is 14% relative (4.2pp vs 4.9pp MAE). This is modest. The coverage improvement (89% vs 83%) is more meaningful but still within the range where a simpler method might suffice with modest adjustments. The question arises: **does the IRT formulation justify its complexity over a well-tuned linear model?**\n\n### 3.4 Honest Framing\n\nThe paper's honest acknowledgment of limitations (wide CIs for RL, signal degradation, identifiability boundaries) is commendable and strengthens scientific credibility. However, this honesty also reveals that the method's sweet spot is narrow: it works well for SFT-contaminated models with good detection signals (AUC >= 0.70) and sufficient model count (N >= 50). This is a specific and somewhat niche use case.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Presentation and Clarity\n\n### 4.1 Structure\n\nThe two-stage pipeline (signal aggregation then IRT calibration) is clearly articulated. The \"when it works / when it doesn't\" framing is effective and should be retained. The paper framing document shows careful thought about positioning.\n\n### 4.2 Table Count\n\n17 tables across 12 pages is excessive. Many can be consolidated or moved to an appendix. Recommendation: 5-6 main-body tables maximum, remainder in supplementary material. The signal survival heatmap (Table 10) is visually compelling and should be a figure rather than a table.\n\n### 4.3 Notation\n\nThe Stan model is clean and readable. However, the paper should provide a formal mathematical presentation of the 4PL+gamma model with clear notation for all variables, not just defer to code.\n\n### 4.4 Experiment Presentation\n\nThe experiment plan is ambitious (13 experiments) but the median-tier imagined results suggest many experiments produce incremental or confirmatory findings. The paper risks burying the key results under too many experiments. Focus the main body on exp-006 (main comparison), exp-008 (signal survival), exp-009 (RL case study), and exp-004 (identifiability).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Weaknesses (Novelty/Positioning)\n\n**W1 (Major): Limited methodological novelty.** The 4PL+gamma model is a minor extension of existing IRT models. Adding a linear covariate to a Bayesian GLM is not a substantial modeling contribution. The paper does not introduce new inference techniques, new theoretical results, or new model structures.\n\n**W2 (Major): Severely inadequate related work.** 10 references is far below the standard for a top venue. Multiple directly relevant papers are missing, including Lalor et al. (AAAI 2024) on IRT for LLMs, the broader item compromise literature, contamination-resistant benchmarks, and alternative evaluation paradigms.\n\n**W3 (Major): Insufficient differentiation from Balachandran et al. [6].** The most directly comparable prior work is not experimentally compared. The claimed differences (IRT vs simpler models, Bayesian CIs) need head-to-head empirical validation.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Narrow operating regime.** The method works well only under specific conditions (SFT contamination, signal AUC >= 0.70, N_models >= 50). For the most practically relevant case (RL-trained models), CIs are ~17pp wide. The honest framing is appreciated but the narrow applicability limits impact.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): Modest improvement over simple baselines.** 14% relative improvement over linear regression + CI does not clearly justify the complexity of the full IRT pipeline. The paper needs a stronger argument for why IRT is necessary rather than merely better.\n\n**W6 (Minor): RRF contribution is negligible.** Delta-AUC of 0.03 over best single signal does not justify claiming signal aggregation as a core contribution.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Strengths (Novelty/Positioning)\n\n**S1: Well-motivated problem and clear framing.** The shift from \"eliminate contamination\" to \"statistically control for it\" is well-argued and the analogy to causal inference is apt. The paper clearly articulates why post-hoc calibration is needed given the failure of mitigation strategies.\n\n**S2: Signal survival analysis is genuinely informative.** The systematic evaluation of which detection signals survive across training regimes is the most novel empirical contribution. The cliff effect for RL post-training is an important finding for the contamination detection community.\n\n**S3: Rigorous identifiability analysis.** The simulation-based parameter sweep (exp-004) establishing minimum signal quality and model count thresholds is a genuine methodological contribution. Knowing when calibration is feasible is arguably more valuable than the calibration itself.\n\n**S4: Principled uncertainty quantification.** The Bayesian framework's ability to propagate detection signal noise into calibration uncertainty is a clear advantage over point-estimate-only baselines. The noise propagation analysis (exp-005) demonstrates this convincingly.\n\n**S5: Dual-track validation design.** The combination of controlled SFT injection (internal validity) and observational RL case study (external validity) is a sound experimental design that strengthens the paper's empirical credibility.\n\n**S6: Honest scope limitation.** The deliberate claim downgrading and explicit identifiability boundaries show scientific maturity. The paper does not oversell.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1**: Why is Balachandran et al. [6] not included as an experimental baseline? This appears to be the most directly comparable prior work. What specific technical limitations prevented a head-to-head comparison?\n\n**Q2**: The 4PL+gamma model treats gamma as a global scalar. This assumes the contamination effect is homogeneous across items and models. Have you considered a hierarchical gamma (gamma_i per model, or gamma_j per item, or gamma_ij)? What happens to identifiability if gamma is heterogeneous?\n\n**Q3**: The paper does not discuss contamination-resistant benchmarks (LiveBench, dynamic evaluation) as an alternative paradigm. In a world where benchmark creators can simply generate new test items, what is the long-term value proposition of post-hoc calibration?\n\n**Q4**: For the RL case study, the 17pp CI width means the calibrated score for a model could range from, say, 55% to 72%. Under what practical decision-making scenario is this informative enough to change behavior? The 17% expected regret reduction is computed under specific assumptions --- how sensitive is this to the choice of decision model?\n\n**Q5**: The IRT model requires N_items >= 300 (per MVP finding). Many important benchmarks have fewer items (HumanEval: 164, GSM8K: ~1300 but with free-form evaluation challenges). How does the method perform on benchmarks with fewer items, and is 300 a hard floor or a soft recommendation?\n\n---\n\n## 8. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Novelty** | 4/10 | The 4PL+gamma model is a straightforward extension of known IRT formulations. RRF is a standard IR technique. Signal survival is the most novel finding but is partially anticipated by prior work. The overall contribution is synthesis, not methodological innovation. |\n| **Significance** | 6/10 | Important problem, useful empirical insights, but narrow operating regime and modest improvement over simpler baselines limit practical impact. |\n| **Related Work & Positioning** | 3/10 | 10 references is severely inadequate. Multiple critical missing citations. Insufficient differentiation from Balachandran et al. No discussion of alternative paradigms (contamination-resistant benchmarks, private evaluation). |\n| **Clarity** | 6/10 | Good high-level structure and honest framing, but too many tables, potential notation issues, and the sheer volume of experiments may obscure the core contributions. |\n| **Overall (R2 perspective)** | 5/10 | Marginally below acceptance threshold. The paper addresses an important problem with a reasonable approach, but the limited novelty, severely inadequate related work, and modest empirical gains over simple baselines do not meet the bar for AAAI. The signal survival analysis and identifiability study are genuine contributions but are supporting rather than core. Strengthening the related work, including Balachandran et al. as a baseline, and providing a clearer argument for why IRT is necessary over simpler models would significantly improve the paper. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I am familiar with IRT models, Bayesian inference, and the LLM contamination detection literature. I am somewhat less familiar with the specific educational measurement item compromise tradition. |\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation\n\n**Marginally below acceptance threshold (5/10).** The paper tackles a real and important problem with a principled statistical approach, and the honest scope-limitation framing is commendable. However, three issues push it below the bar: (1) the core model is a minor extension of existing IRT-with-covariates formulations rather than a genuine methodological advance; (2) the related work section with only 10 references is far below AAAI standards and misses critical citations; (3) the 14% improvement over linear regression raises the question of whether the full IRT pipeline is justified. The signal survival analysis and identifiability mapping are the paper's strongest novel contributions --- the authors might consider repositioning the paper around these findings rather than the calibration framework itself.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1743,"cache_read_input_tokens":81038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1743,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0147H16AmgeJ9qKA5FfTAu8A","session_id":"239c4b18-ea77-4d79-9faf-bb096f3c14d6","uuid":"57d4888a-6905-4bc7-a199-2c0ca90a49db"}
